  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Potential for frost tonight inland from the\ncoast and north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe Frost Advisory has been expanded to include interior Hancock\nand central Washington counties. Surface 1040mb high pressure \nis centered over Quebec this afternoon, with upper level high \npressure building into the region from Ontario. This pattern \nwill continue tonight, resulting in clear skies and light winds,\nsimilar to last night. Once again this should result in a cold \nnight, frost, and river valley fog. A modifying air mass could \nlimit how low temperatures fall tonight, but lows in the 30s are\nexpected outside the Downeast Coast. Northern areas of Maine \nwill likely reach freezing or below freezing. While not as cold\nas tonight, Thursday night could see additional patchy frost \nand fog, mainly in northern areas as clouds increase from the \nsouth.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal low will impact at least Downeast \nMaine late this week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA coastal low is expected to develop across the Mid-Atlantic \nregion late this week while high pressure remains centered over \nQuebec. The timing and track of the ocean low is still uncertain\nwith blocking high pressure to the northwest of our forecast \narea and ridging across the Great Lakes. Latest ECMWF guidance \ndelays the onset of rain into the weekend as the blocking high \nremains resolute over Eastern Canada. This will result in \nadditional uncertainty in how close the low will pass to the \ncoast which will determine how far north the precipitation will \nreach. Regardless, northerly to northeasterly winds will become \ngusty over the weekend, especially along the coast, as a tight \npressure gradient forms. General trend currently is that the low\npressure system will continue to move southwards due to the \nhigh pressure, with majority of the rain staying over the waters\nand the Downeast region.",
